	<p><font face="verdana, arial, helvetica" size="2" ><blockquote><font face="verdana,arial,helvetica" size="1" >quote:</font><hr><br />
You are judged by the company you keep.<br />
<hr></blockquote><br />
<br />
Ok, now I gotta drag Tsabrae into the mosh pit for a bit.<br />
<br />
1) Who are you to judge me?<br />
2) Why should I care?<br />
<br />
This is not directed at you personally as much as it is a reminder that your judgement of another person is irrelevant in most cases.<br />
<br />
If you were trying to buy a car from me, then it might matter.<br />
<br />
If you were going to work for me, it might matter.<br />
<br />
If you were going to join my guild, then it might matter.<br />
<br />
Since you and I, in this case, have had, nor are likely to have, any interaction in EverQuest, why is your judgement the least bit important?  How informed is that judgement?  Have you ever had any direct interaction with me?  Do you know the first thing about me?  Why would you need to?<br />
<br />
Let's use Vindication as an example.<br />
<br />
There are a lot of folks who have judged them based on third hand evidence. (evidence which is mostly reliable, but that's not always the case)  Did you know anyone in that guild? Did you have any interactions with them in the game?<br />
<br />
Why should they care what you think?<br />
<br />
This is nothing more than an illustration of a problem that many folks (particularly Americans) have in an age when anyone and everyone's opinion can be broadcast to a wide audience. (Internet, Talk Radio, etc)<br />
<br />
Sure, you have an opinion, but why should anyone care?  What difference will your opinion make?  Unlike many other rhetorical questions that I ask, this is not an implication that your opinion makes no difference.  It depends on the situation and the forum.<br />
<br />
In this particular forum, for this particular game, expressing an opinion over another guild or even another individual means next to nothing.  It's been demonstrated time and time again.  "Everybody knows" what bastards Vindication were, yet there are some genuinely nice folks who were in that guild, and have gone on to become good friends of mine. (Hi Zarx, Teut, Van, and many other ex-Vindi Hosslings)  There are others who were in that guild that I am convinced via direct interaction  are booger eating morons (Hi Shik, Hallinforth...)<br />
<br />
Net result, the same damn thing everyone always says about a guild.  There are good folks and bad folks in each one.<br />
<br />
I'll give ya one more ironic little factoid...<br />
<br />
50 bonus points to the person who can name the individual that I've played online RPG's with longer than anyone else, (irony?  He's in VE)  I'll give you a hint, we both played on a Star Wars MUD together back in the early 90's. (about 5 years before EQ existed)<br />
<br />
The point is, judgement based on a person's friends may be adequate in some superficial cases, but it doesn't tell the whole story.<br />
<br />
Hell, I was a Rutabaga for almost a year, and I don't think you could find two more radically different people than me and Alihja. (who I love dearly) <br />
<br />
-Grim</font></p>
	
